<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Hingston Down</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">838</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Hingston Down was fought in 838 at Hingston Down in east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tamar">River Tamar</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht of Wessex</a> defeated a combined army of Cornish Britons and Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>. Recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, it is the last recorded battle between the Cornish and the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and is seen as the end of effective Cornish independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hingston-down.jpg" alt="Battle of Hingston Down" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philg88; Attribution: Wikimedia Foundation (www.wikimedia.org) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_British_Isles_9th_century.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
